  {
    "id": "10.1038/s41893-026-01981-x",
    "venue": "natsustain",
    "year": 2026,
    "track": null,
    "title": "Photocatalytic nitrogen reduction to high-concentration ammonia via interfacial evaporation",
    "authors": [
      "Bowen Lv",
      "Xinfei Fan",
      "Yuanlu Xu",
      "Yanbin Xin",
      "Chengwen Song",
      "Yanming Liu"
    ],
    "author_ids": [],
    "abstract": "Photocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (PNRR) offers a potentially sustainable route for green ammonia (NH 3 ) synthesis, but its practical implementation is limited by sluggish N 2 and active hydrogen (*H) supply, high reaction barriers and product inhibition from NH 3 accumulation on the catalyst surface. Here we show a cyanobacteria-inspired photocatalytic–photothermal interface that integrates PNRR with solar-driven interfacial evaporation (SDIE), coupling NH 3 synthesis with in situ product separation. The resulting PNRR–SDIE platform accelerates reaction kinetics and mass transport while continuously removing NH 3 from the catalytic interface, yielding an NH 3 concentration of 2,462.38 µmol l −1 in the collected condensate water under simulated solar irradiation, 5.43–6.27 times higher than in conventional PNRR systems. Mechanistic studies reveal that the synergistic triple-phase interface and localized photothermal heating lower the activation energy for N 2 reduction and facilitate reactant transport, while rapid NH 3 desorption and evaporation suppress product inhibition and sustain continuous synthesis. We further demonstrate the practical utility of this platform for enhancing Brassica rapa growth using the generated NH 3, establishing a closed-loop route from solar energy to NH 3 and agricultural productivity. This work provides a strategy for coupling photocatalytic synthesis with interfacial evaporation for decentralized solar production and simultaneous separation of molecular products.",
